    {
      "title": "docs: one template for every benchmark page, and a chart above every sweep",
      "source_type": "github_pr",
      "url": "https://github.com/prog893/mlx-asr/pull/10",
      "excerpt": "Restructures every page in `docs/benchmarks/` to one template, and charts every sweep. All charts are drawn from the tables already published.\n\n## Template\n\nLever and engine pages:\n\n1. **Title**, prefixed `Lever:` or `Engine:`.\n2. **Conclusion**, then a `setting | default | why` table.\n3. **Setup:** one line that links [corpus.md](docs/benchmarks/corpus.md) instead of re-describing the corpus.\n4. **One section per experiment**, with the chart directly above its table.\n5. **Optional sections:** How it works, Superseded, Not settled.\n6. **Related.**\n\nReference pages (corpus, metrics, determinism, peak-memory) use a lighter layout.\n\n\"ships\" is gone everywhere, docs and charts alike. Every option is offered; the docs name the **default**.\n\n## Corpus described once\n\n`corpus.md` now carries stable anchors (`#the-20-file-corpus`, `#the-7-file-subset`, `#the-single-clip`, `#timed-references`). No page keeps its own Corpus section.\n\nIt also states what the timed set actually is, which two pages had wrong: the single clip plus six published videos that are part of the 20 files.\n\n## Charts\n\nThere are 29 charts now, up from 7, and every sweep table has one. Each chart is light/dark SVG, uses the validated palette, distinct markers, and direct end labels from four series up.\n\n- **Per-page data:** each page's chart data lives in `scripts/docs/sweeps/<page>.py`. The page owns it, so pages can be edited independently.\n- **Row matching:** `tests/test_sweeps.py` matches each chart row to its table row: row-wise, or column-wise for the config-by-threshold `min_cut` tables.\n- **Honest axes:** memory axes start at 0, and ties get a minimum y span so a difference inside noise does not draw as a slope.\n- **Links:** `tests/test_doc_links.py` checks that every relative link and `#anchor` in the docs resolves.\n- **Freshness:** the existing chart tests still compare the committed SVGs byte for byte against a fresh render.\n\n## Content fixes found while restructuring\n\nEach was checked against code or source:\n\n- qwen3-asr's window is 30s; one line said 60s (`models.py`).\n- Voxtral's 6.77GB peak was measured at the old 60s/B16 profile; the current Ultra profile is 30s/B32.\n- `--prompt` exits 2 on other engines. The page said it was \"reported as ignored\" (`cli.py`).\n- No-condition applies to Whisper `small` and up only.\n- Machine labels use nameplate GiB: 16GB and 128GB, not 17GB and 137GB.\n- determinism.md now lists every greedy engine.\n- The qwen3-asr precision ladder is described as measured everywhere, including the CLI help.\n\nRestructured by one agent per page in parallel, then integrated and checked centrally: full suite 1072 passed.\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n\n\n<!-- This is an auto-generated comment: release notes by coderabbit.ai -->\n## Summary by CodeRabbit\n\n* **Documentation**\n  * Expanded benchmark guides with updated results, model recommendations, setup details, and clearer explanations of accuracy, speed, memory, and configuration trade-offs.\n  * Added responsive charts with light- and dark-mode support across benchmark pages.\n  * Improved navigation to detailed engine reports, reference metrics, and corpus information.\n* **Bug Fixes**\n  * Corrected outdated benchmark links and clarified measured defaults and limitations.\n<!-- end of auto-generated comment: release notes by coderabbit.ai -->",
